+NOTES = r"""
+Definition and theorem boundary. Section 1 defines a cover as a subset of
+hyperedges covering every vertex. Section 2 defines d-semi-ladders and proves
+Theorem 1: H is (d+1)-semi-ladder-free exactly when the longest inclusion chain
+in its intersection closure has length at most d+1. Every generated edge has
+one endpoint in each of two parts. Its closure has only V, two-element edges,
+singleton intersections and the empty set, so it has chain length at most 3
+and is 2-flat. The solver receives and verifies the actual hypergraph; there is
+no graph/CSP surrogate replacing the paper's object.
+
+STEP 0 hardness decision. Theorem 3 gives an FPT algorithm for d-Flat Set Cover
+in O(k^(d*k) * k * ||H||), and Theorem 4 gives an O(n^(2d+2)) kernelization.
+Moreover, on this deliberately 2-uniform bipartite subclass a cover of n edges
+is exactly a perfect matching, so Hopcroft-Karp solves it in polynomial time.
+A Track A claim would be false. This module therefore declares Track B and
+measures Hopcroft-Karp as its reference algorithm. The compact route uses the
+finite-field labels: all edges in one generated layer have a constant endpoint
+quotient. One supplied anchor inverse plus n modular multiplications recovers
+an entire matching. Generation samples the multipliers first and composes their
+bijections; it never invokes matching.
+
+What the paper makes easy. Small k is explicitly FPT by Theorem 3, and the
+kernel of Theorem 4 has size O(k^(d^2)). The lower bound in Theorem 7 concerns
+compression size for fixed d >= 3; it is not a distributional hardness theorem
+and does not apply to this d=2 matching subclass. Theorem 1, not the lower
+bound, is the construction license used here.
+
+Attack controls. Every left and right vertex has the same degree and every
+multiplier layer is generated identically; the stored answer chooses one layer
+uniformly, while verify accepts every perfect cover. Presentation is resampled
+only until the named local/greedy probes fail, without searching for a cover.
+The adversary panel checks degree/ID outliers, smallest-right greedy matching,
+256 structure-aware random restarts, and a local smallest-integer-gap ansatz.
+The successful domain algorithm is reported separately, as Track B requires.
+canonical_key ignores list indices and edge order and uses the two incidence-
+side neighborhood-intersection multisets together with the normalized set of
+intrinsic field-label quotients. These are exact invariants under independent
+input reorderings. It is not a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form; that
+limitation is recorded in the README.
+""".strip()
